<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zengid dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Zengid, Zengids</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Zengid dynasty was a Turkic dynasty that ruled northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in the twelfth century, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imad-al-din-zengi">Imad al-Din Zengi</a>, who captured Edessa from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> in 1144. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a> extended Zengid power and sent forces into Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>, who went to Egypt in Zengid service, ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zengid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Zengid dynasty" width="320" height="238" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: naturalearthdata.com, offered to the Public Domain per Terms of Use · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zengid_territory.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
